WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:04.929
Okay, so now that we've had a look at some of the light types

2
00:00:04.929 --> 00:00:06.509
and some of the basic properties of lights,

3
00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:08.250
let's go ahead and take a look at one of the lighting systems

4
00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.470
of Unreal Engine 5, which is going to be Lumen.

5
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.529
So the topics that we're going to cover in this video

6
00:00:13.529 --> 00:00:16.030
are what is Lumen, how we set it up,

7
00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.070
some quality control to make sure that we get the best out of Lumen,

8
00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.250
some tips and tricks, as well as some visualizers

9
00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:25.899
that we can take a look at to troubleshoot some of the issues

10
00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:27.640
that we might have with Lumen.

11
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.980
So when it comes to what Lumen is,

12
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.399
it's Unreal Engine 5's fully dynamic global illumination

13
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.060
and reflection system, which is designed for the next-gen consoles

14
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.920
as well as high-end PCs.

15
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.640
Now, what's really cool about Lumen is that you're not dependent

16
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.100
on high-end ray tracing GPU

17
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:47.100
to get ray tracing, essentially.

18
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.600
And another thing that's really cool about Lumen

19
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.479
is that it renders diffuse into reflection with infinite bounces

20
00:00:52.479 --> 00:00:55.399
and indirect specular reflections in loud detail environments

21
00:00:55.399 --> 00:00:57.979
at scales ranging from millimeters to kilometers.

22
00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:01.719
Now, the thing, and the important thing about that

23
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:03.740
is it provides infinite diffuse bounces,

24
00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.629
which means when it comes to the light bleed in between

25
00:01:06.629 --> 00:01:10.049
or the color bleed in between surfaces in Unreal,

26
00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:13.310
we aren't limited to a particular number of bounces.

27
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.010
We have infinite bounces for that, which is really cool.

28
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:18.010
Now, another thing that's great about it

29
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.849
is that when used in combination with Nanite's virtualized geometry,

30
00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:25.849
it allows us to have very, very, very detailed geometry

31
00:01:25.849 --> 00:01:27.849
and also achieve full resolution shading

32
00:01:27.849 --> 00:01:31.689
while computing that indirect lighting cheaper than ever before.

33
00:01:33.519 --> 00:01:36.780
Skylighting is also solved as part of Lumen's final gather process,

34
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.780
and that includes sky shadowing,

35
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.859
which allows for indoor spaces to be much darker than outdoor lighting,

36
00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:46.340
and you get much more natural-feeling effects.

37
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.799
And another thing to keep in mind is that it also provides

38
00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:52.420
slightly lower quality, lower illumination for light translucency

39
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.260
and volumetric fog, although that's improving as we speak,

40
00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:00.680
and we hope that future releases actually make this really, really cool.

41
00:02:02.379 --> 00:02:05.500
Another cool feature about Unreal is that it supports emissive materials

42
00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.719
at no additional performance cost.

43
00:02:07.719 --> 00:02:11.340
There is a limit to how small and bright the objects can be

44
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.599
that emit light, so it's actually always a little bit better

45
00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:18.139
to use actual lights than this gear is.

46
00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.900
Another thing to keep in mind is troubleshooting these things

47
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.740
that can be harder to solve than manually placed lights,

48
00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.930
but it is still a really cool feature to have,

49
00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:34.020
especially for larger meshes.

50
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.520
When it comes to reflections,

51
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.439
it solves indirect specular or reflections

52
00:02:43.439 --> 00:02:46.319
for the full range of the material's roughness value,

53
00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:48.900
and another great thing is that the fused golden animation

54
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.659
in the shadow skylight can be seen in reflections,

55
00:02:51.659 --> 00:02:53.919
as well as being supported in clear coat materials,

56
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:56.819
which is really, really cool.

57
00:02:56.819 --> 00:02:58.819
What does it replace, then?

58
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.259
It replaces the skylight with ambient occlusion

59
00:03:02.259 --> 00:03:04.259
or unshadowed skylights.

60
00:03:04.259 --> 00:03:06.259
It replaces any form of screen space

61
00:03:06.259 --> 00:03:08.259
global illumination system that we might have had.

62
00:03:08.259 --> 00:03:13.020
It replaces full-on hardware ray tracing global illumination,

63
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.849
as well as any third-party global illumination solutions.

64
00:03:16.849 --> 00:03:19.229
Now, how do we set this up?

65
00:03:19.229 --> 00:03:22.229
To set Lumen up, you just need to make a few changes

66
00:03:22.229 --> 00:03:25.289
on the project settings.

67
00:03:25.289 --> 00:03:28.050
On the project settings, you want to go to the Rendering tab

68
00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.689
and make sure that the dynamic global illumination system

69
00:03:30.689 --> 00:03:34.120
is set to Lumen, that the reflections and their method

70
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.979
are set to Lumen as well.

71
00:03:35.979 --> 00:03:37.539
And this should happen automatically,

72
00:03:37.539 --> 00:03:39.659
but if it doesn't, just make sure you double-check

73
00:03:39.659 --> 00:03:42.780
the Generate Mesh Distance fields is on.

74
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.439
And another thing that you might need to do

75
00:03:44.439 --> 00:03:47.479
is change your default RHI to DirectX 12

76
00:03:47.479 --> 00:03:49.840
so that you can have Shader Model 6 enabled.

77
00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:54.009
This is required for some of the visualizers.

78
00:03:54.009 --> 00:03:56.009
From here on, any other changes that we want to do

79
00:03:56.009 --> 00:03:58.550
down the line for changing quality and things like that

80
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.830
will be done via the post-process volume.

81
00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.199
Now, if we take a look at some of the things

82
00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:07.580
that we can do to get a slightly better reflections,

83
00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.379
if you have a good enough card,

84
00:04:09.379 --> 00:04:12.419
is one cool thing about Lumen is that it can form

85
00:04:12.419 --> 00:04:17.139
a little bit of a hybrid system with actual ray tracing.

86
00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:22.240
So you can set up your reflections to be ray traced.

87
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:27.019
So to get better reflections,

88
00:04:27.019 --> 00:04:29.360
and if your card can handle it, as I say,

89
00:04:29.959 --> 00:04:32.560
you can change these settings to change the ray lighting mode

90
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.860
to hit lighting for reflections,

91
00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:42.139
and the software ray tracing mode to detail tracing.

92
00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:43.439
When it comes to quality control

93
00:04:43.439 --> 00:04:47.019
and how we can change the quality for performance

94
00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:52.110
in terms of Lumen's scene quality settings,

95
00:04:52.269 --> 00:04:53.850
we can go to the post-process volume

96
00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:54.889
and change the following things.

97
00:04:54.889 --> 00:04:57.889
So if we change the Lumen scene lighting quality,

98
00:04:57.889 --> 00:05:01.490
it scales the actual quality of Lumen.

99
00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:04.329
So larger scales on this will mean

100
00:05:04.329 --> 00:05:08.129
that the Lumen scene is calculated with higher fidelity,

101
00:05:08.129 --> 00:05:10.490
which will be visible in the reflections, for example,

102
00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.209
but the larger the value, the higher the GPU cost.

103
00:05:13.209 --> 00:05:14.490
And you'll hear me say this a lot,

104
00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.370
especially when we start tweaking values

105
00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.519
to get better detail.

106
00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:21.759
The Lumen scene detail controls the size of instances

107
00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:23.560
that can be calculated in the scene.

108
00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:25.519
So the larger the value,

109
00:05:25.519 --> 00:05:28.879
the smaller the objects that can be calculated,

110
00:05:28.879 --> 00:05:31.860
but again, at the cost of GPU.

111
00:05:31.899 --> 00:05:34.699
The Lumen scene view distance,

112
00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:36.779
so if you set the maximum view distance

113
00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:43.000
so that the scene maintains, sorry,

114
00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:45.199
that sets the maximum view distance of the scene

115
00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:47.120
that Lumen maintains for ray tracing against,

116
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.560
the large values will help with sky shadowing

117
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.639
and global illumination, but again, at the cost of GPU.

118
00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:54.980
And then we have the Lumen final gather quality

119
00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.860
which scales Lumen's final gather quality,

120
00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:02.040
and again, the larger the values, the less noise,

121
00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.269
but the higher the GPU cost.

122
00:06:06.269 --> 00:06:08.470
Now, some more advanced settings here

123
00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:12.870
are the lighting update quality settings.

124
00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:16.149
So if you change the Lumen quality update speed,

125
00:06:16.149 --> 00:06:21.209
it will control how much Lumen scene is allowed to cache

126
00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:23.870
the lighting results that help with performance.

127
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:24.970
So the larger the value,

128
00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.889
the faster those changes are gonna propagate,

129
00:06:26.889 --> 00:06:29.050
but the higher the GPU cost.

130
00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:31.610
And when it comes to the final gather lighting update speed,

131
00:06:31.649 --> 00:06:33.490
it controls how much Lumen final gather

132
00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.329
is allowed to cache those lighting results,

133
00:06:35.329 --> 00:06:36.689
just like before.

134
00:06:36.689 --> 00:06:38.889
And again, the larger the values,

135
00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:41.610
the faster those changes are gonna propagate,

136
00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.959
but again, at the cost of your GPU.

137
00:06:45.959 --> 00:06:48.120
When it comes to reflections,

138
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.120
to get higher quality reflections,

139
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.079
you wanna change under the Lumen reflection sections,

140
00:06:53.079 --> 00:06:56.000
if you change the values of the quality here,

141
00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.959
it will reduce the noise in the reflections,

142
00:06:58.959 --> 00:07:00.959
however, higher GPU cost.

143
00:07:00.959 --> 00:07:03.680
And then one tip is to change the ray lighting mode

144
00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.360
to hit lighting for reflections,

145
00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.399
and that'll be the highest quality,

146
00:07:07.399 --> 00:07:10.680
but again, increase GPU cost.

147
00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.879
Now, some visualizers that you might find handy

148
00:07:13.879 --> 00:07:15.399
to troubleshoot some of the issues

149
00:07:15.399 --> 00:07:17.079
that you might be having can be found.

150
00:07:17.079 --> 00:07:18.040
So under the show tab,

151
00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.939
you've got under the Lumen section here,

152
00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:22.560
a lot of information that you can take

153
00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.740
to actually see and visualize any of these changes.

154
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:29.120
And then if you go under the lit or the view mode,

155
00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:30.240
yeah, if you go to Lumen,

156
00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.600
you can also see the overall Lumen scene,

157
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:33.879
how the reflections are doing

158
00:07:33.879 --> 00:07:37.509
and what the surface cast is doing.

159
00:07:37.509 --> 00:07:39.230
Now, to get into some tips and tricks

160
00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:43.860
that you might find handy when it comes to using Lumen.

161
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:45.980
So Lumen does not currently support

162
00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.180
fully mirrored reflections.

163
00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.959
It's on the roadmap, but currently is limited.

164
00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.089
It does not support any static lights,

165
00:07:54.089 --> 00:07:55.329
which I think would make sense

166
00:07:55.329 --> 00:07:58.810
considering that we are using

167
00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.980
a fully dynamic global illumination system.

168
00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:03.420
Now, however, one cool thing

169
00:08:04.019 --> 00:08:06.019
is that if you do disable a lot of static lighting,

170
00:08:06.019 --> 00:08:07.899
it can help with some of the overhead

171
00:08:07.899 --> 00:08:09.420
and it makes sure your Lumen AR process

172
00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.180
is working with no hindrances.

173
00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:13.889
Now, another cool thing is that Lumen

174
00:08:13.889 --> 00:08:15.709
does support material and inclusion,

175
00:08:15.709 --> 00:08:18.540
which is really good for characters.

176
00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.800
It does not currently support world position offsets.

177
00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.120
So if you have it active in a project,

178
00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.560
any of those materials that are offset

179
00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.240
inverts through the material

180
00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.699
will become a little bit blurry in the movement.

181
00:08:29.699 --> 00:08:31.420
It does support all physical lights,

182
00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:33.940
but as long as the mobility of those lights

183
00:08:34.379 --> 00:08:36.950
is set to movable.

184
00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.990
And a couple of cool little tricks,

185
00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:41.250
especially if you're trying to soften your shadows,

186
00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.850
is the source angle on the main directional light

187
00:08:43.850 --> 00:08:46.269
that will change and soften your shadows

188
00:08:46.269 --> 00:08:49.240
for that dominant directional light.

189
00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.379
And if you change the source radius,

190
00:08:51.379 --> 00:08:52.659
the larger the source radius,

191
00:08:52.659 --> 00:08:55.149
the softer your shadows are going to be.

192
00:08:55.149 --> 00:08:58.029
So those are some tips and tricks that you can find

193
00:08:58.029 --> 00:09:00.509
handy when working with Lumen.

194
00:09:00.509 --> 00:09:04.149
Okay, so here's some additional resources if you need them.

195
00:09:04.149 --> 00:09:05.830
After this, we'll be taking a look at Nanite

196
00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:07.490
and its virtual shadow map.
